### What is cache-aside (lazy loading)?

Cache-aside, also called lazy loading, is the most common read strategy. On a read, the application checks the cache. On a miss it loads from the database and populates the cache, so only requested data is cached. Its weakness is that the first read is slow and, without invalidation, the cache can go stale after a database write.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CacheAsideBalance {
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        BigDecimal cached = cache.get(accountId);
        if (cached != null) return cached;          // cache HIT

        BigDecimal balance = database.loadBalance(accountId); // MISS -> DB
        cache.put(accountId, balance);              // populate cache
        return balance;
    }
}
```

### What is write-through caching?

Write-through writes to the cache and the database together on every write, so the cache is always consistent with the database. Writes are slightly slower because two systems are updated, but reads never see stale data. This is my default for balances in banking, where correctness beats a little write latency.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class WriteThroughBalance {
    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
        database.save(accountId, newBalance);   // durable first
        cache.put(accountId, newBalance);       // then cache (consistent)
    }
}
```

### What is write-behind (write-back) caching?

Write-behind writes to the cache immediately and queues the database write to happen asynchronously. Writes are very fast and can be batched, but if the cache node crashes before flushing, recent writes are lost. This makes write-behind unsuitable for money and acceptable only for tolerant data like analytics counters or view counts.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class WriteBehindCounter {
    public void incrementViewCount(String pageId) {
        cache.increment(pageId);                // fast, in-memory
        asyncFlushQueue.enqueue(pageId);        // DB write happens later
        // If cache crashes before flush, recent counts are lost
    }
}
```

### How does cache invalidation work?

Invalidation keeps the cache correct. A time-to-live lets entries expire automatically, so stale data self-heals after a bounded delay. This is simple but allows brief staleness. Event-based invalidation deletes or updates the cache entry the moment the underlying data changes, which is precise but requires wiring the write path to the cache.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class InvalidationStrategies {
    // TTL-based: entry expires after 60 seconds
    public void cacheTtl(String key, String value) {
        cache.put(key, value, Duration.ofSeconds(60));
    }

    // Event-based: invalidate immediately on data change
    public void onBalanceChanged(String accountId) {
        cache.delete(accountId);  // next read will reload from DB
    }
}
```

### What is cache stampede and how do you prevent it?

Cache stampede, also called the thundering herd, happens when a very popular key expires and many requests simultaneously miss the cache and all hit the database at once. Prevention techniques include a single-flight lock so only one request rebuilds the value while others wait, and staggered or probabilistic early expiration so the key is refreshed before it fully expires.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class StampedeProtection {
    private final Lock rebuildLock = new ReentrantLock();

    public String getWithProtection(String key) {
        String value = cache.get(key);
        if (value != null) return value;

        if (rebuildLock.tryLock()) {          // only one thread rebuilds
            try {
                value = cache.get(key);       // double-check after lock
                if (value != null) return value;
                value = database.load(key);   // one DB call, not thousands
                cache.put(key, value);
                return value;
            } finally {
                rebuildLock.unlock();
            }
        }
        // Other threads wait briefly and retry
        return waitAndRetry(key);
    }
}
```

### How do you compare these strategies?

| Strategy | Read speed | Write speed | Consistency | Durability risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Cache-aside | Fast (hit) / Slow (miss) | N/A (read-only strategy) | Can go stale | None |
| Write-through | Always fast | Slower (two writes) | Always fresh | None |
| Write-behind | Always fast | Very fast | Always fresh | Data loss on crash |

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class StrategyComparison {
    public String recommend(String dataType) {
        return switch (dataType) {
            case "account_balance" -> "Write-through + event invalidation (correctness)";
            case "analytics_counter" -> "Write-behind + TTL (speed, loss OK)";
            case "product_catalog" -> "Cache-aside + short TTL (read-heavy, rarely changes)";
            default -> "Cache-aside + TTL (safe default)";
        };
    }
}
```

### When should you use which strategy in banking?

For banking balances and ledger data, use write-through with event-based invalidation. The cache is always consistent, and any balance change is immediately reflected. For analytics counters and non-critical metrics, write-behind is fine because losing a few seconds of counts on a crash is acceptable. For product catalogs and reference data that change rarely, cache-aside with a short TTL is simple and effective.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that these strategies combine. Cache-aside reads with write-through or event-based invalidation and stampede protection on the hottest keys gives both speed and correctness. They also know that cache consistency is harder in distributed systems where multiple application instances can race to update the same cache key, and that techniques like cache-aside with delete-on-write (rather than update-on-write) reduce the window for race conditions.
